\u00a318.0m forecast) was financed from existing resources and reserves, so no external borrowing arose from capital activity and the council reports full compliance with the gross-borrowing-vs-CFR prudential indicator. Investments were managed entirely in-house, growing from \u00a390.3m to \u00a3108.7m over the year, and returned 4.62% against a 3.86% benchmark, beating the original budget for investment interest by \u00a3918,000 (and the February 2026 revised forecast by \u00a392,000). The council deliberately kept cash balances thin and used reserves rather than external borrowing to fund capital spend, which the report frames as both cheaper (avoiding the borrowing/investment rate differential) and lower-risk (less counterparty exposure). The 4-page report circulated does not include the Annex containing the full prudential-indicator tables, so ratio-of-financing-costs and CFR figures themselves are not available to ground here.","fy":"2025/26","as_of_doc":"Treasury Management Outturn 2025/26","as_of_url":"https://testvalley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s40677/Treasury Outturn Report 2025-26.pdf","as_of_date":"2026-06-24","signals":[{"code":"none","headline":"No external borrowing in 2025/26, gross-borrowing-vs-CFR indicator complied with, and investment income beat original budget by \u00a3918,000 (\u00a35.318m actual vs \u00a34.4m budget)","why":"A council funding all its capital spend from reserves rather than debt, and outperforming its investment income budget, carries materially lower near-term financial risk than one drawing down reserves or borrowing to bridge gaps."}],"key_figures":[{"name":"capital expenditure","value":"\u00a312.5m actual vs \u00a318.0m forecast (2024/25 actual was \u00a39.0m); fully financed, \u00a30.0m unfinanced","as_at":"2025/26 outturn","page":2},{"name":"investment principal","value":"\u00a3108.7m principal at 31/03/26, up from \u00a390.3m at 31/03/25, all internally managed","as_at":"31 March 2026","page":2},{"name":"la lending","v